
# about to be public, and git history keeps what you delete.
# cargo-fuzz working state. The targets are committed; the corpus and the crashes
# Cargo.lock IS committed, deliberately. The old advice not to commit it for a
# committing it makes CI reproducible, lets `cargo audit` see exact versions, and
dist/
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
fuzz/artifacts/
fuzz/corpus/
fuzz/coverage/
fuzz/target/
# NOT ignored, deliberately: `hivecomb-node/index.js` and `index.d.ts` are the
# addon's entry point and its types. napi generates them at build time, and they
# used to be ignored on the reasoning that generated files do not belong in git.
# But the release job publishes without building -- it only downloads prebuilt
# `.node` artifacts -- so they were absent from the checkout and `hivecomb@0.1.0`
# went to npm containing nothing but a README, a LICENSE and a package.json
# pointing at a `main` that was not there. Committing them is the napi-rs
# convention for exactly this reason. CI checks they match a fresh build.
.idea/
# it finds are not — a crash artifact belongs in a bug report, not in git.
# library is out of date: it is ignored by downstream consumers either way, and
# makes the declared MSRV mean something -- without it, dependencies resolve to
*.node
# Node addon build output and dependencies
node_modules/
package-lock.json
# ...except the addon's, which pins @napi-rs/cli -- the tool that *generates* the
# committed `index.js` and `index.d.ts`. Without it CI resolved a newer napi than the
# working tree, generated a different entry point, and three CI jobs failed on the
# difference. Committing a generated file while leaving its generator unpinned is
# incoherent; this is the other half of that decision.
!hivecomb-node/package-lock.json
__pycache__/
*.pyd
**/*.rs.bk
# Secrets. Nothing holding a private key may ever be committed — this repo is
*.so
# Any cargo target directory, at any depth. This was `/target`, anchored to the
# repository root, which missed `benches/rust-libraries/target` -- a second
# workspace outside this one -- and 1887 build artifacts were committed before
# anyone noticed.
target/
# setuptools output, for the same reason. `python/build/lib/beem` was a tracked
# copy of `python/beem` that had already drifted from it -- account.py and cli.py
# differed -- so a grep or a reader could land on the stale one. Regenerated by
# every build; never the source of anything.
build/
*.egg-info/
.venv/
wallet.json
# whatever is newest and the floor moves on its own.
*.wif

# Drafts written for you, not for the repository: the npm support correspondence
# and the Hive post. They live in the working tree and are deliberately not
# tracked -- a `git add -A` swept them in once, which is what this prevents.
npm-support-*.md
artifact.md
